{"id":"cluster-administration-system-logs","corpus":"k8s","title":"System Logs","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/system-logs/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"System Logs"},{"kind":"p","text":"System component logs record events happening in cluster, which can be very useful for debugging. You can configure log verbosity to see more or less detail. Logs can be as coarse-grained as showing errors within a component, or as fine-grained as showing step-by-step traces of events (like HTTP access logs, pod state changes, controller actions, or scheduler decisions)."},{"kind":"p","text":"In contrast to the command line flags described here, the log output itself does not fall under the Kubernetes API stability guarantees: individual log entries and their formatting may change from one release to the next!"},{"kind":"h2","text":"Klog"},{"kind":"p","text":"klog is the Kubernetes logging library. klog generates log messages for the Kubernetes system components."},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes is in the process of simplifying logging in its components. The following klog command line flags are deprecated starting with Kubernetes v1.23 and removed in Kubernetes v1.26:"},{"kind":"li","text":"--add-dir-header"},{"kind":"li","text":"--alsologtostderr"},{"kind":"li","text":"--log-backtrace-at"},{"kind":"li","text":"--log-dir"},{"kind":"li","text":"--log-file"},{"kind":"li","text":"--log-file-max-size"},{"kind":"li","text":"--logtostderr"},{"kind":"li","text":"--one-output"},{"kind":"li","text":"--skip-headers"},{"kind":"li","text":"--skip-log-headers"},{"kind":"li","text":"--stderrthreshold"},{"kind":"p","text":"Output will always be written to stderr, regardless of the output format. Output redirection is expected to be handled by the component which invokes a Kubernetes component. This can be a POSIX shell or a tool like systemd."},{"kind":"p","text":"In some cases, for example a distroless container or a Windows system service, those options are not available. Then the kube-log-runner binary can be used as wrapper around a Kubernetes component to redirect output. A prebuilt binary is included in several Kubernetes base images under its traditional name as /go-runner and as kube-log-runner in server and node release archives."},{"kind":"p","text":"This table shows how kube-log-runner invocations correspond to shell redirection:"},{"kind":"h3","text":"Klog output"},{"kind":"p","text":"An example of the traditional klog native format:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The message string may contain line breaks:"},{"kind":"h3","text":"Structured Logging"},{"kind":"p","text":"Migration to structured log messages is an ongoing process. Not all log messages are structured in this version. When parsing log files, you must also handle unstructured log messages."},{"kind":"p","text":"Log formatting and value serialization are subject to change."},{"kind":"p","text":"Structured logging introduces a uniform structure in log messages allowing for programmatic extraction of information. You can store and process structured logs with less effort and cost. The code which generates a log message determines whether it uses the traditional unstructured klog output or structured logging."},{"kind":"p","text":"The default formatting of structured log messages is as text, with a format that is backward compatible with traditional klog:"},{"kind":"p","text":"Example:"},{"kind":"p","text":"Strings are quoted. Other values are formatted with %+v, which may cause log messages to continue on the next line depending on the data."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Contextual Logging"},{"kind":"p","text":"Contextual logging builds on top of structured logging. It is primarily about how developers use logging calls: code based on that concept is more flexible and supports additional use cases as described in the Contextual Logging KEP."},{"kind":"p","text":"If developers use additional functions like WithValues or WithName in their components, then log entries contain additional information that gets passed into functions by their caller."},{"kind":"p","text":"For Kubernetes 1.37, this is gated behind the ContextualLogging feature gate and is enabled by default. The infrastructure for this was added in 1.24 without modifying components. The component-base/logs/example command demonstrates how to use the new logging calls and how a component behaves that supports contextual logging."},{"kind":"p","text":"The logger key and foo=\"bar\" were added by the caller of the function which logs the runtime message and duration=\"1m0s\" value, without having to modify that function."},{"kind":"p","text":"With contextual logging disable, WithValues and WithName do nothing and log calls go through the global klog logger. Therefore this additional information is not in the log output anymore:"},{"kind":"h3","text":"JSON log format"},{"kind":"p","text":"JSON output does not support many standard klog flags. For list of unsupported klog flags, see the Command line tool reference."},{"kind":"p","text":"Not all logs are guaranteed to be written in JSON format (for example, during process start). If you intend to parse logs, make sure you can handle log lines that are not JSON as well."},{"kind":"p","text":"Field names and JSON serialization are subject to change."},{"kind":"p","text":"The --logging-format=json flag changes the format of logs from klog native format to JSON format. Example of JSON log format (pretty printed):"},{"kind":"p","text":"Keys with special meaning:"}],"lemmas":{"systems":["system","systems"],"logs":["log","logs"],"logged":["logged","log"],"records":["record","records"],"recorded":["recorded","record"],"recording":["recording","record"],"events":["event","events"],"happening":["happening","happen"],"happenings":["happening","happenings"],"happens":["happens","happen"],"